      "fieldData": {
       "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Conditional Logic Form FAQ\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a conditional logic form?\", \"a\": \"A conditional logic form changes as someone fills it in. An answer can show a follow-up question, skip a page, decide who receives the response, or pick the final page. Each rule pairs a trigger with an action, such as asking for a company name only when someone says they are buying for work.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a conditional logic form?\", \"a\": \"Write down the questions, then each rule as a plain sentence, and describe both to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>. Emergent builds the form from that prompt, with its logic and a database for the responses. When you find a rule you missed, add it with another prompt and the form changes to match.\"}, {\"q\": \"Which free form builder has the best conditional logic?\", \"a\": \"No single builder wins for every form: pick the one whose free version handles the rules you need. Tally's help center says conditional logic is available for free to all Tally users. Jotform says conditional logic is available on all Jotform plans, including the free plan.\"}, {\"q\": \"How can I use conditional logic in forms?\", \"a\": \"Use it to show a follow-up only when an answer calls for it, to skip pages that do not apply, and to send each response to the person who handles it. In a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/multi-step'>multi step form</a>, a rule can also move someone past several steps at once. A rule can change the last page too: a large order can end on a different message than a small one.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are good examples of conditional logic forms?\", \"a\": \"Good conditional logic forms let one answer change the rest of the form. A wedding RSVP asks for a meal choice only from guests who are attending. A support form asks for an order number only when the question is about billing. A rental inquiry skips the pet questions when the applicant has no pets.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can a conditional logic form check a typed answer instead of a multiple choice one?\", \"a\": \"In some form builders, yes. Tally's help center says you can add conditions on any type of input block. Google's help center says going to a section based on an answer is only available for Multiple choice and Dropdown question types. Before you pick one, list the typed answers your rules depend on, such as a number of guests or a ZIP code.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I put a conditional logic form on a landing page?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Emergent can build the page and the form from one prompt, and a video landing page can end with a short form whose next question depends on the first answer. Keep that form to three or four questions, and let the rules hide anything a viewer does not need to answer.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I see which path each person took through a conditional logic form?\", \"a\": \"Look at the answers that trigger your rules. Every response from a form built with Emergent is saved with all its answers in a database you own. Filter by the answer that drives a rule, such as everyone who picked delivery, and you have the list of people who took that path.\"}, {\"q\": \"Why is my conditional logic form not working?\", \"a\": \"Check where each rule sits and which question it points at. Tally's help center lists the most common issues: the logic block is placed on the wrong page, conditions reference the wrong field, or the form hasn't been tested with all answer combinations. After you rename an answer option, check every rule that mentions it.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can a conditional logic form change its questions based on where someone came from?\", \"a\": \"Yes, when the link carries that information. Give each source its own link, such as one ending in ?source=booth for a trade show, and write a rule that reads it. Someone who arrives from a qr code landing page can then skip the question about how they heard of you. In Emergent, that rule is one sentence in your prompt.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
      }
     }
